  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nShowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast this weekend, \nwith the possibility of 1-3 inches of rain and a few strong/severe\nstorms.\n\nLow pressure over the Mid-Mississippi Valley will move slowly \neastward into KY/TN this weekend with influx of moisture laden\nair into the Appalachians...PWATs increasing into the 1.50 to\n2.00 inch range for our forecast area. This will promote\nscattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms, beginning this\nafternoon/evening and continuing through the day Sunday with \nprimary focus along the southern Blue Ridge where Convective \nAllowing Models (CAMs) suggest S-SE upslope flow will enhance \nthe rain efficiency and provide for an area of low level lift \nand regeneration. \n\nAs the shortwave trough drives the low closer to our region, \ndynamic lift will also lead to sustainable rain production even\nwith loss of daytime heating, probability for rainfall just as\nhigh tonight as it will be during the day Sunday. \n\nGreatest hazards through the weekend will be two-fold. \n\nThe first hazards is heavy rainfall, especially given how moist\nwe expect the airmass to be across the forecast area. The storm\nsystem will be rather slow-moving, allowing for the rain to be \npersistent. The development of off-and-on showers and storms \nover similar areas could result in localized ponding/flooding \nissues. As such, WPC has our area outlined for a Marginal, or \nLevel 1 Risk, of excessive rainfall through Sunday with a \nSlight, or Level 2 Risk, of excessive rainfall for the southern \nBlue Ridge from Patrick and Carroll Counties in VA into the \nNorth Carolina High Country and Northwest Piedmont of NC.\n\nThe second hazard is the possibility of isolated strong or \nsevere thunderstorms. The right entrance region of the upper jet\nshould be moving across our neck of the woods Sunday afternoon,\nresulting in increased diffluence aloft. Thermodynamically, \nmixed-layer CAPE values around 1000 J/kg (maybe a little more) \ncould seen, especially from Southside Virginia into the North \nCarolina Piedmont. With the increased forcing provided by the \nsurface low and cold front to our west, we could see some \nisolated strong or severe storms in this environment. There are \na couple of limiting factors in my mind... (1) Boundary layer \nwind speeds might be a tad low to get significant storm-scale \norganization. (2) If precipitation is more widespread than \ncurrently forecast, then instability should be lower. Given the \noverall risk, SPC has our area outlined for a Marginal Risk \n(Level 1) for seeing severe storms Sunday with the primary \nimpact being damaging winds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUnsettled weather continues through the front half of the week,\nwith a possible decrease in convective coverage at the end of the\nweek.\n\nAs an upper-level low settles into the Tennessee Valley, it should \nslowly push a cold front through most of our area before it stalls. \nThe upper low is then expected to \"sit and spin\" close to the \nforecast area through much of the upcoming week. This feature will \ncontinue to send disturbances through a rather moist airmass, \nresulting in continued odds of seeing daily showers and \nthunderstorms. There's some indication that convective probabilities \nmay decrease during the latter half of the week as the upper low \nejects toward the Northeast U.S.",
